From 4a19812636acd34249f76079a194b22306d2df8e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: logdog2325 <loganjayfisher@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 9 Aug 2026 23:20:50 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] Battle Style: patch the link path's models, not the story
 path's

The field test proved the model writes were byte-perfect and running -- in the
same block as music and venue writes that visibly worked, including loading the
never-used Phenac and Orre colosseums -- and still did nothing. Ralf's modifier
sites serve the story and deck-trainer paths; GBA-link battles never read them.

Disassembly of a clean main.dol found the real chain. At handshake the game
turns each save's game+gender into a virtual trainer id 0x1389..0x138E and
stores it in a fixed per-side .bss struct; the battle scene maps that id to a
model through a switch. So: any of the six GBA player models is a pure 16-bit
pin of the side's id (upper halfword zero -- it is a repeat count, and nonzero
would clobber the neighbouring ordinal). Any other model routes the side
through one of the two Emerald arms and patches that arm's li -- host through
the male arm, guest through the female one, and when only one side takes an
arm the other side's id is pinned to its natural value so a same-gender custom
save cannot wander into the patched arm.

The model table's tiers now say what is actually known: the six GBA models are
the verified tier, and every other model is experimental until the link
scene's people archive proves it can load them. The staging copies of both
sides are pinned as well as the live structs, closing the one write-ordering
race the disassembly could not exclude. Side mapping (slot 0 = host) is the
single remaining device check; if reversed, the two sides' constants swap.

Version 1.2.2.
